# About Slamhaus

Slamhaus is a small, opinionated web tool for people who think in systems,
tools, levels, simulations, and source files. It keeps content in Markdown and
puts browser behavior in typed, game-oriented C++.

## The idea

Most static-site tools are built around a scripting ecosystem. Most frontend
tools assume the browser is the centre of the universe. Slamhaus takes a third
route: treat a website like a buildable project.

Pages are source files. Routes are data. Components are declarations. C++ is an
optional behavior module. The compiler turns those pieces into a complete,
inspectable website without asking a runtime to stay alive after deployment.

## The build pipeline

The pipeline is deliberately visible: each stage has a narrow job and produces
an inspectable hand-off for the next one.

```slam-ui
Column BuildPipeline() {
  class = "pipeline-grid";
  Element SourceStep() {
    tag = "div";
    class = "pipeline-step pipeline-source";
    Text SourceKicker("01 / source");
    Text SourceLabel("Markdown + .slam");
  }
  Element ParseStep() {
    tag = "div";
    class = "pipeline-step";
    Text ParseKicker("02 / understand");
    Text ParseLabel("parse + validate");
  }
  Element RenderStep() {
    tag = "div";
    class = "pipeline-step";
    Text RenderKicker("03 / compose");
    Text RenderLabel("route + render");
  }
  Element CompileStep() {
    tag = "div";
    class = "pipeline-step pipeline-optional";
    Text CompileKicker("04 / optional");
    Text CompileLabel("C++ → Emscripten");
  }
  Element ShipStep() {
    tag = "div";
    class = "pipeline-step pipeline-output";
    Text ShipKicker("05 / ship");
    Text ShipLabel("atomic dist/");
  }
};
```

The result above is a real Slam UI component, not a decorative image. Its
layout is responsive CSS, its labels are generated semantic HTML, and its
optional WebAssembly stage is shown as part of the same flow rather than as a
separate product story.

## A game-developer's web stack

Slamhaus borrows useful instincts from game development:

- explicit data flow instead of ambient global state;
- named systems with narrow responsibilities;
- deterministic builds and reproducible toolchains;
- small value types and clear ownership at boundaries;
- a fast edit-check-build loop;
- an escape hatch when the built-in primitive is not enough.

It does not pretend to be an engine. There is no scene graph, reflection
system, garbage collector, proprietary renderer, or fake browser abstraction.
The SDK gives you useful browser capabilities and leaves rendering choices to
you.

## Designed for real sites

Slamhaus validates missing pages, route collisions, unsafe paths, partial
cycles, unsupported behavior adapters, and invalid component properties before
replacing output. Generated HTML includes semantic regions, labels, live output,
focus states, and responsive defaults. The result can be served from a folder,
an object store, a CDN, or GitHub Pages.

## The honest trade-offs

Slamhaus is a good choice if you value readable source, a small toolchain, C++
behavior, and a static deployment. It is not trying to replace a CMS, a mature
JavaScript application framework, or a visual page builder. If you need a huge
plugin marketplace or a client-side state graph with hundreds of screens,
another tool may be the better fit.

## Project status

The project is young and intentionally experimental, but its contracts are
treated seriously: stable diagnostics, shared language specifications, pinned
toolchains, automated tests, generated API documentation, and examples that
exercise the real compiler.

Start with the [syntax guide](syntax.md), browse the [examples](examples.md),
or inspect the [live showcase](showcase.slam) and [API cheatsheet](syntax.md).

